<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · city</p><h1 class="enc-title">Taunggyi</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Taunggyi is the capital of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shan-state">Shan State</a> in eastern Myanmar, set on the Shan Plateau at about 1,400 metres above sea level, near <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/inle-lake">Inle Lake</a>. Under British rule it was the seat of the superintendent and then commissioner of the Federated Shan States, and it grew into the main administrative and trading town of the region. It is known for its hot-air balloon festival held each November.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/taunggyi.jpg" alt="Taunggyi" width="320" height="180" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Heinz_Htetz · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Shwe_Bone_Pwint_Pagoda,_Taunggyi,Shan.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Setting</h2><p>Taunggyi lies at an elevation of 1,436 metres on a high basin among the mountains of eastern Myanmar, and unlike many towns it is built along the mountain ridges rather than on a valley floor. Its name means big mountain in Burmese and refers to a ridge on its east, part of the Shan Hills, whose high point is called Taung-chun, the Spur. The city has a humid subtropical climate with a dry winter from December to March and a wet summer from April to November, and it is known as a cool hill station. It is 635 kilometres from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yangon">Yangon</a> by road.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>Before the town was founded, villages such as Mon Che, Hum Bwa and Oo See stood in the area under the Nyaungshwe sawbwas. After the British annexation of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/upper-burma">Upper Burma</a> in 1885 the colonial administration first put its headquarters for the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shan-state">Shan States</a> at Maing Thauk (Fort Stedman) on <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/inle-lake">Inle Lake</a>, but malaria and the damp, low ground made it unsuitable. In 1894 the offices moved to the higher plateau where the village of Taunggyi stood, and the town was planned as a hill station on a grid of streets. By 1906 it housed about 1,000 buildings and was the residence of the British Superintendent of the Southern Shan States.</p><p>After independence in 1948 Taunggyi remained the capital of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shan-state">Shan State</a>. Its position on the road to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kengtung">Kengtung</a> and the Thai border made it a junction for trade, and migration brought a mixed population of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pao-people">Pa-O</a>, Shan, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/intha">Intha</a> and Burmese-Chinese communities.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">The place today</h2><p>Taunggyi is the capital of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shan-state">Shan State</a> and its administrative, commercial and educational centre, with several universities. National Highway 4 links it with Meiktila to the west and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kengtung">Kengtung</a> and the Thai border to the east, while the nearest airport is at Heho. The city has Buddhist monasteries, the cathedral of the Roman Catholic Archdiocese of Taunggyi, a Baptist church, mosques and Chinese temples, and a Shan State Cultural Museum. The Tazaungdaing festival, which marks the end of the rainy season, is marked by the Taunggyi Hot Air Balloon Festival. Inle Lake and the Kekku Pagodas, with hundreds of stupas dating from the 16th century, lie nearby.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Taunggyi” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
